[WIP][RED] tests: detect a Playwright browser, fall back to local ARP

Feature 1 — browser detection.  `pip install playwright` installs no
browser; the binaries come from a separate `playwright install`.  Today the
fetcher hard-codes `playwright.chromium.launch()`, so a machine that only
ran `playwright install firefox` dies on a missing executable.  The tests
pin the replacement: probe `browser_type.executable_path` for each of the
three engines, then use the first one that exists, preferring the engine
named by `--browser`/`PW_BROWSER`.

Feature 2 — degrade instead of dying.  `ARPTableFetcher.available()` already
exists but is never consulted by `FetcherChain`, so a source that cannot
possibly run (no browser installed) is still called and still raises.  The
tests pin: an unavailable source is skipped without being called, the chain
reports "unavailable" when every source is, and `default_fetcher_chain()`
still lands on the OS-independent local ARP table.

Also pins `--browser` validation at parse time and `build_fetcher` passing
the chosen engine through to the fetcher.

12 failures, all for the intended reasons (no `my_lan_prober.browsers`
module; no `installed_browsers` hook; `available()` ignored by the chain).
